{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said to Balaam.</b> It is necessary to know why it was necessary to say <b>to Balaam</b> and it would not have sufficed to say ״and He said to him.״ Further, why was it necessary to say <b>you shall not go, you shall not curse.</b> Further, why was it necessary to tell him the reason <b>for blessed is he.</b> And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Numbers Rabbah 20:9), and this is their language: He [Balaam] said to Him, ״I will curse them in Your name״; He said to him, ״You shall not curse״; he said to Him, ״If so, I will bless them״; He said to him, ״for blessed is he״ — end quote." "And it appears to explain the verse according to what I explained in the statement ״who are these men״ etc., that He [HaShem] intended to say to him why he considered them worthy to bring them into his room, and he answered him that Balak etc. — in this manner, for HaShem answers him point by point: regarding what he answered ״Balak son of Zippor etc. sent to me״ and they are the king's emissaries — therefore the blessed statement comes ״you shall not go with them,״ meaning, they are not worthy for him to go with them according to the measure of his stature. And He informed him that Balak had demeaned his honor and did not send him many and distinguished princes, and this is what was intended in the first statement <b>who are these men</b> — a expression of lowliness, not from the side of the sender but from the side of the one sent, namely Balaam, [who] did not understand the intent at first. And you will find that Balaam understood the matter and said to the princes ״HaShem refuses to let me go with you,״ and Balak added by sending many and distinguished princes." "<b>And the reason</b> that HaShem considered him [worthy] and paid him this honor, besides the reason I wrote above in the verse ״who are these men״ etc., it further appears to give a reason for the matter: that HaShem gave him his reward, for he said at the beginning of his reply to the elders of Moab: ״and I will bring you back word as HaShem speaks to me,״ and it is known that the Holy One, blessed is He, does not withhold the reward of any creature, even of the nations of the world. Go out and learn (Sanhedrin 96b) what the Holy One, blessed is He, paid to Nebuchadnezzar for his steps, etc.; and here too He paid him his reward and honored him and said to him ״you shall not go with them,״ meaning, a statement touching upon Balaam's honor: ״you shall not go with them,״ that this is not your honor even though they came to call you for a matter you have permission to do. And therefore He was precise in saying <b>with them</b>, and corresponding to the essence of the mission He said ״you shall not curse the people.״" "And He said <b>you shall not curse</b> [תאור], and did not use Balaam's own language, who said ״curse [קבה] for me,״ — to inform him that He also knew the language of the mission that Balak sent, and He used the language of cursing [ארה], and thereby needled Balaam with this hint, that He knew he had altered his language." "And He said <b>for blessed is he</b>, so that he would not understand that the two statements HaShem said to him — ״you shall not go״ and ״you shall not curse״ — share one and the same reason, namely Balaam's honor, as we deduced from His saying ״to Balaam״ and from His saying the word ״with them.״ For according to this, when there are many and distinguished princes he would do Balak's will and go and curse. Therefore He said <b>for blessed is he</b>, meaning, this is the reason for the statement ״you shall not curse.״ And as for the matter of going with them, that is for the reason of insisting on Balaam's honor." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to explain the verse in this manner: since HaShem knew that Balaam was eager to do evil and would ultimately go — as indeed turned out to be the case, for HaShem consented and sent him, as I shall explain the reason in its place with HaShem's help — therefore HaShem said to him <b>you shall not go with them</b>, meaning ״with them״ — He left the going open to him. For had He said to him ״you shall not go,״ there would be no opening left to return and say to him ״arise, go.״" "And He was precise in saying <b>to Balaam</b>, intending to give the reason for the matter: because of Balaam's scheme, for he wanted to go — therefore it is that He did not firmly forbid him the going. And He was precise in saying ״with them.״" "And His saying <b>you shall not curse</b> etc. <b>for they are blessed</b>, He intended by this to respond to what Balaam scrutinized in his words and said ״come now, curse״ etc., according to what I explained, that he meant to say that the claim of Moab was a claim whose time of merit in Moab had not yet arrived now. Therefore He said <b>you shall not curse</b> etc. And if you say: but has the time of their merit in Moab not arrived? — ״for they are blessed״: ברוך means nothing other than צדיק (righteous), for righteousness is the source of blessings. And the intention in this is that they should not be suspected of wickedness, for surely they would not enter the category of those whose time has not yet arrived. And He chose to designate the righteous one as ברוך (blessed), thereby intending to include as well what we explained in the first manner."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:43.852567939Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}